[0.567, "o", "\u001b[H\u001b[1;38;5;212m› Can you show me how to compute fibonacci sequence recursively in python\u001b[0m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\r\nFibonacci fits recursion naturally: each number is the sum of the two before it, and the recursion stops at zero and one. I'll build the plain recursive function first, then add caching so\r\nrepeated work is eliminated, and finish with how to run it.                                                                                                                                 \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\nThe function below is a direct translation of the definition. When `n` is zero or one it returns `n` itself, which acts as the base case, and any larger value recurses on the two previous \r\nnumbers.                                                                                                                                                                                    \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\n\u001b[38;5;241m│ \u001b[0m\u001b[38;5;252mdef fib(n):\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                                                               \r\n\u001b[38;5;241m│ \u001b[0m\u001b[38;5;252m    if n < 2:\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                                                             \r\n\u001b[38;5;241m│ \u001b[0m\u001b[38;5;252m        return n\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                                                          \r\n\u001b[38;5;241m│ \u001b[0m\u001b[38;5;252m    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                                        \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\nThe plain version is fine for small inputs, but it recomputes the same values over and over, which makes it exponential in time. Wrapping it with `functools.lru_cache` stores every result,\r\nso each `n` is computed only once and the whole thing runs in linear time.                                                                                                                  \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\n\u001b[38;5;241m│ \u001b[0m\u001b[38;5;252mfrom functools import lru_cache\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                                           \r\n\u001b[38;5;241m│ \u001b[0m\u001b[38;5;252m\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                                                                          \r\n\u001b[38;5;241m│ \u001b[0m\u001b[38;5;252m@lru_cache(maxsize=None)\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                                                  \r\n\u001b[38;5;241m│ \u001b[0m\u001b[38;5;252mdef fib(n):\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                                                               \r\n\u001b[38;5;241m│ \u001b[0m\u001b[38;5;252m    if n < 2:\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                                                             \r\n\u001b[38;5;241m│ \u001b[0m\u001b[38;5;252m        return n\u001b[0m                                          "]
[0.000, "o", "                                                                                                                                \r\n\u001b[38;5;241m│ \u001b[0m\u001b[38;5;252m    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                                        \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\nOn Python 3.9 and later you can use `@functools.cache` instead; it behaves identically for a function like this with no arguments needing eviction. Put the function in a file, then add a  \r\nsmall snippet that calls it to check the output.                                                                                                                                            \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\n\u001b[38;5;241m│ \u001b[0m\u001b[38;5;252mif __name__ == \"__main__\":\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                                                \r\n\u001b[38;5;241m│ \u001b[0m\u001b[38;5;252m    print([fib(i) for i in range(10)])\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                                    \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\nRunning the file prints the first ten numbers of the sequence in one line. Call `fib(30)` or higher and it returns instantly thanks to the cache.                                           \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\n\u001b[38;5;241m│ \u001b[0m\u001b[38;5;252m# fib.py\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                                                                  \r\n\u001b[38;5;241m│ \u001b[0m\u001b[38;5;252mprint(fib(30))  # 832040\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                                                  \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\n                                                                                                                                                                                            \r\n     "]
